            0.381,
            0.13
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "FASCINATING FACTS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.134,
            0.739,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "Ancient Indian astronomers, including Aryabhata, had also noticed the daily apparent motion of the celestial objects, such as the Sun, Moon, planets and stars. Aryabhata was a famous mathematician and astronomer of ancient India who wrote an important treatise, Aryabhatiya, around the fifth century CE. The apparent motion of the stars due to the rotation of the Earth is explained in Verse 9, Golapada, Aryabhatiya."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.256,
            0.517,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "¥ÙéÜæð×»çÌÙæñüSÍÑ ÂàØˆØ¿Ü¢ çßÜæð×»¢ ØmÌ÷ïÐ"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.281,
            0.519,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": "¥¿ÜæçÙ ÖæçÙ ÌmÌ÷ï â×Âçp×»æçÙ ÜVæØæ×÷ïH"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.307,
            0.738,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "Just as a man in a boat moving forward sees stationary objects as moving backwards, so also the stars that are stationary are seen by people of Lanka as moving towards the west."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.357,
            0.738,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "Aryabhata’s stated value for the time taken by the Earth to complete one full rotation about its axis is around 23 hours 56 minutes 4.1 seconds (in modern units). This value is impressively close to the currently accepted value."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.737,
